omial Setting","position":{"start":{"line":67,"column":1},"end":{"line":67,"column":1}},"key":"Bq4kcPszW8"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-recognizing-a-binomial-setting","label":"Check Your Understanding: Recognizing a Binomial Setting","html_id":"check-your-understanding-recognizing-a-binomial-setting","implicit":true,"key":"rujDaOwntb"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-2894e134e783774470136a55fde586bb.mjs","model":{"question":"A basketball player makes 70% of her free throws. She shoots 15 free throws in practice, and X is the number she makes (assume shots are independent). Is X binomial?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Yes—binomial with $n = 15$ and $p = 0.7$","feedback":"Correct! Fixed $n = 15$ trials, independent shots, success/failure outcomes, and constant $p = 0.7$."},{"correct":false,"text":"No—the number of trials is not fixed","feedback":"The number of trials IS fixed at 15."},{"correct":false,"text":"No—there are more than two outcomes per shot","feedback":"Two outcomes (make/miss) is exactly what binomial requires."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Check the four requirements: fixed n, independence, two outcomes, constant p.","explanation":""},"key":"prTNz8VWSb"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-2894e134e783774470136a55fde586bb.mjs","model":{"question":"A city has 50,000 registered voters, 60% of whom support a ballot measure. A pollster samples 500 voters at random without replacement; X is the number of supporters in the sample. Can X be treated as binomial?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Yes, approximately—the sample is far less than 10% of the population","feedback":"Correct! The sample (500) is only 1% of the population (50,000), so the selections are virtually independent and X is approximately binomial with $n = 500$, $p = 0.6$."},{"correct":false,"text":"No—sampling without replacement always breaks independence","feedback":"Strictly the trials are dependent, but the rule of thumb (n $\\leq$ 0.10N) says the dependence is negligible here."},{"correct":false,"text":"No—p is unknown","feedback":"A binomial variable needs a probability p, and here $p = 0.6$ is given."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Apply the 10% rule of thumb: is 500 at most 10% of 50,000?","explanation":""},"key":"ceCO6v3Ywm"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-2894e134e783774470136a55fde586bb.mjs","model":{"question":"You flip a coin until you get 3 heads, and X is the number of flips required. Is X binomial?","choices":[{"correct":false,"text":"No—each flip has more than two outcomes","feedback":"The two outcomes per flip aren't the issue here."},{"correct":true,"text":"No—the number of trials is not fixed","feedback":"Correct! The number of trials is not fixed in advance—it depends on when the third head appears—so X is not binomial."},{"correct":false,"text":"No—p changes from flip to flip","feedback":"p is constant at 1/2 for every flip; the problem is the unfixed number of trials."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Is the number of trials fixed in advance?","explanation":""},"key":"n90kU9Ntdz"}],"key":"WJ6qXABlLo"}],"key":"KaEj6NG5TU"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Adding Random Variables: Sums of Means and Variances","url":"/pages/p143-m11-rules-for-means-and-variances-of-random-v","group":"Section 8: Random Variables"},"next":{"title":"Building the Binomial Probability Formula","url":"/pages/p145-m11-binomial-random-variables-2-of-4","group":"Section 8: Random Variables"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}